GMime fuzzing
=============

Adapted from MimeKit's Fuzzing/Targets/MimeReaderTarget.cs,
MimeParserTarget.cs, EncodersTarget.cs, DecodersTarget.cs, Rfc2047Target.cs,
AddressParserTarget.cs, HeaderValuesTarget.cs, SmtpUtf8ValidatorTarget.cs,
Helpers/{Fuzz,RecordingMimeReader,MimeComplianceChecker,
MimeRoundTrip}.cs and corpus-seeding/replay scripts. The six hand-written
MIME seeds and mime.dict are copied byte-for-byte from MimeKit's Fuzzing
directory. These files retain their upstream MIT license in LICENSE.
No MimeKit reader implementation or changed diagnostic locations are imported.

Targets
-------

mime-reader checks callback ranges and normalized callback traces with and
without a compliance logger, and with fragmented input. Consecutive read
callbacks are folded into FNV-1a hashes plus lengths, as in MimeKit, so
legitimate buffer segmentation does not cause false mismatches. It also checks
diagnostic positions, contexts, metadata and per-violation budgets, comparing
diagnostic multisets per message across chunk sizes (independent validators'
delivery order may vary with buffering). Both standalone messages and mbox
are scanned.

mime-parser compares byte-exact serialized output from memory-backed,
source-backed/fragmented, and logged parsing. GMime has no async parser API,
so storage and chunking comparisons replace MimeKit's sync/async comparisons.
All runs use the public GMimeParser, including its compliance options and
logger APIs for the logged run.
Checks include mbox markers/progress, repeated writes, retained source-content
lifetimes and parse/write fixed points. Like MimeKit, the latter permits
convergence solely by removal of bare CRs in malformed input.

encoders exercises the public Base64, quoted-printable, uuencode, yEnc and
pass-through codecs. It checks whole/chunked and clone/reset/flush equivalence,
output estimates and exact stores, alphabets and line lengths, GLib Base64
equivalence, reference CRC-32, and direct and stream-filter round trips.
The first byte selects the codec modulo five; the second selects line length
and LF/CRLF and the whole input seeds deterministic, bounded chunk sizes.
Seven-byte calls force Base64's scalar path alongside the dispatched path.
UU buffers whole lines even with small chunks; an independent scalar sextet
reference checks its output instead.

decoders checks malformed Base64, quoted-printable body/header modes, full-block
and payload-only uuencode/yEnc, and pass-through input. It compares whole,
split, cloned, reset and seven-byte scalar-sized decoding, checks output
guards, and round-trips through four matching encoders. The low three bits
of the first byte select the decoder and the upper five select the split.
GMime's private RFC 2231 and one-shot RFC 2047 payload codecs are not invoked
directly; the public header utilities exercise RFC 2047 payload encoding.
Unlike MimeKit's padded SIMD stores, every GMime codec path must leave bytes
after the returned output length untouched.

rfc2047 exercises strict/loose text and phrase decoding, UTF-8 output and
expansion bounds, UTF-8 text encode/decode round trips, and phrase encoding.
As in MimeKit, round trips exclude control characters and trim outer spaces;
GMime's existing all-space pass-through behavior is preserved.
Literal NULs are mapped to spaces for GMime's public C-string APIs so the
remaining input is still exercised; encoded NULs remain unmodified.

structured-headers directly exercises checked address/list/mailbox/group,
source-route, parameter, Content-Type and Content-Disposition parsers, their
private raw-header recovery paths, and Message-ID/References scanners.
Each input and selected prefix/suffix slices are copied into exact-size
allocations, with no added terminator (even empty slices end at the allocation
boundary). Results are compared with the same bounded bytes followed by an
out-of-range suffix, and checked NUL-free inputs also with C-string parsing.
Comparisons include display text, parameter values and formatting metadata,
errors, warning payloads and identifier cursor positions. Round-trip checks
adapt MimeKit's AddressParserTarget and HeaderValuesTarget (e607255b): encoded
addresses must converge with only display-name whitespace/IDNA normalization,
and content fields may take one extra round to settle encoded-word charsets
without changing parameter names or values. Address convergence allows eight
non-shrinking rounds; shrinking rounds remain bounded by the output length.
Plausible identifiers must survive angle-bracket formatting, and dates must
retain both their instant and UTC offset. These checks do not impose strict
round trips on the identifier parser's deliberately malformed recovery output.
The target also checks input preservation, reference progress and identifier
UTF-8. Literal NULs are preserved: checked APIs must reject them, while raw
recovery still processes the complete range. A whole-input selector varies all
three address/parameter
compliance modes, domainless/unquoted-comma address options and strict/loose
RFC 2047 decoding without consuming syntax. Round-trip formatting uses LF.
The harness reuses production GMime objects to reach private parsers without
exporting them, including when the normal library is shared.

smtp-address-validator checks the public SMTP and SMTPUTF8 validators with all
twelve option combinations. It compares exact-size non-terminated input with
bounded and NUL-terminated copies, verifies boolean/error agreement and input
preservation, and checks that length limits, ASCII-as-Latin and IDN decoding
only change results as documented. Raw invalid UTF-8 and NUL bytes remain
unmodified, replacing MimeKit's UTF-16/lone-surrogate input path.

Reader/parser whole-input selectors leave MIME prefixes unchanged. Input
limits are 1 MiB for reader/parser/decoders, 256 KiB for encoders and 64 KiB
for rfc2047/structured-headers, and 4 KiB for smtp-address-validator.
Both replay and libFuzzer enforce them. MIME
depth is limited to 16, address-group depth to 4, and mailbox operations
to 10,000 messages per run. Reaching the mailbox work budget is recorded in
the compared traces, not misreported as a parser defect. Only documented
parse/header-limit failures are accepted; other errors and GLib
warnings/criticals terminate the process.

Build and replay (Linux shell commands)
--------------------------------------

Any supported C compiler can build the replay harness; Python 3 generates
the deterministic regression corpora:

  meson setup _build-replay -Dfuzzing=replay
  meson compile -C _build-replay
  meson test -C _build-replay --suite fuzzing --print-errorlogs
  _build-replay/fuzzing/gmime-fuzz --list
  _build-replay/fuzzing/gmime-fuzz --replay all fuzzing/corpus

Replay accepts files and recursively enumerated directories, reports slow
inputs (over two seconds), and fails on unreadable/missing paths or when
no eligible inputs were replayed. A failing input aborts immediately; its
path is printed before execution. Oversized files are counted as skipped.

Coverage-guided fuzzing
----------------------

Use a separate Clang build. Coverage instrumentation is applied to GMime
itself, not just the harness. These binaries are for testing, not installation.
Clang's compiler-rt/libFuzzer runtime must be installed.

  CC=clang meson setup _build-fuzz -Dfuzzing=libfuzzer \
    -Ddefault_library=static \
    -Db_sanitize=address,undefined -Db_ndebug=false \
    -Dcrypto=disabled -Dlibidn=disabled -Dintrospection=disabled \
    -Dvapi=disabled -Dgtk_doc=false -Dexamples=false -Dtests=false
  meson compile -C _build-fuzz
  meson test -C _build-fuzz --suite fuzzing --print-errorlogs
  python3 fuzzing/seed-corpora.py _build-fuzz/artifacts

The seeder merges the hand-written seeds, existing round-trip fixtures and
up to 200 messages per test mailbox, deduplicated by SHA-256. It never deletes
an existing working corpus. Keep generated corpora and crash artifacts out
of the source tree (build directories are already ignored).

Codec seeds cover all decoder selector bits, encoder options, binary payloads,
SIMD/line/chunk boundaries, malformed escapes and MIME line tails. Header seeds
include MimeKit's five RFC 2047 examples and UTF-8, whitespace and NUL edges.
Structured-header seeds cover comments, escaped quotes, groups, source routes,
RFC 2231 continuations and charsets, encoded words, malformed identifiers,
literal/decoded NULs, every prefix of the syntax examples and allocation-size
edges through the 64 KiB limit. The full seeder also extracts folded structured
and address header values from message/mbox fixtures, following MimeKit's
fuzz-seed-corpora.ps1 without decoding or truncating embedded NULs.
Meson generates these deterministic regression seeds at build time; the full
seeder additionally imports tests/data/encodings fixtures with codec prefixes.
SMTP seeds include the checked-in MimeKit test vectors and every short prefix,
malformed UTF-8/NULs, ISO-2022-JP, length boundaries and long combining sequences.

Run each target separately:

  ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1:halt_on_error=1 \
  UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:print_stacktrace=1 \
  _build-fuzz/fuzzing/fuzz-mime-reader \
    -dict=fuzzing/mime.dict -max_len=1048576 -timeout=10 \
    -rss_limit_mb=2048 -max_total_time=600 -print_final_stats=1 \
    -artifact_prefix=_build-fuzz/artifacts/mime-reader/crashes/ \
    _build-fuzz/artifacts/mime-reader/corpus

For encoders/decoders use codecs.dict instead of mime.dict. Use -max_len=262144
for encoders and -max_len=65536 for rfc2047/structured-headers; other targets use
1048576 except smtp-address-validator, which uses -max_len=4096.
Use structured-headers.dict for the structured-headers target, and
smtp-address-validator.dict for smtp-address-validator.
gmime-fuzz --list prints each target's input limit.

Replay a crash using the matching executable and crash filename, without
a corpus directory, or use gmime-fuzz --replay. libFuzzer's -minimize_crash=1
can reduce it before adding a permanent regression test. Preserve the seed,
command, compiler/sanitizer configuration and failure output when triaging.

GitHub Actions
--------------

.github/workflows/fuzz.yml follows MimeKit's nightly/manual fuzz workflow.
It runs all seven targets nightly at 03:17 UTC for 600 seconds each. Manual runs
can select one target and a duration from 1 to 14,400 seconds. Each job
replays the regression seeds, restores and extends a target/branch-specific
corpus cache, then saves a new immutable cache even after a failure.
Crashing inputs, fuzzer output and Meson logs are uploaded on failure.
